n principles using controlled numerical experiments. 1 Probability-flow ODEs in diffusion models Score-based diffusion models [ 19 ] generate data by reversing a noising process and may be sampled either with a reverse-time SDE [ 3 , 5 ] or with the deterministic probability-flow ODE (PF-ODE) introduced in Song et al. [18] . The PF-ODE is attractive because it removes stochastic simulation, supports deterministic encoding/decoding, and permits likelihood calculations [ 19 , 18 ] . In constrained generative modeling, reflected diffusion models [ 15 , 12 ] keep the forward process inside a prescribed domain and can likewise be paired with a deterministic PF-ODE. The formal derivation of the PF-ODE in these references is based on the observation that, for the diffusion d ​ X t = f ⁡ ( X t , t ) ​ d ​ t + σ ⁡ ( t ) ​ d ​ W t , \mathrm{d}X_{t}=f(X_{t},t)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sigma(t)\,\mathrm{d}W_{t}, (1) the marginal density p t p_{t} satisfies the Fokker–Planck equation ∂ t p t + ∇ ⋅ ( f ​ p t ) − 1 2 ​ σ ​ ( t ) 2 ​ Δ ​ p t = 0 . \partial_{t}p_{t}+
